in recent years Brazil has gone through  a rollercoaster of turmoil between  economic woes corruption scandals and  impeachment proceedings Brazil is in a  deep crisis many blaming fingers point  to President Dilma Rousseff who is  recently suspended from office but this  has been a slow unraveling for the  world's fifth most populous country so  here's the situation in order to  understand Rousseff you need to start  with her predecessor President Luiz  inácio Lula da Silva aka Lula  rags-to-riches story  Lula was wildly popular and became a  symbol for Brazil's economic extent as  one of the top exporters in the world of  commodities such as oil soy beans corn  and iron ore Dilma Rousseff was Lula's  protege and became the first woman to be  elected president of Brazil in 2010  during Rousseff's presidency global  prices for raw materials began dropping  and a corruption scandal paralysed many  infrastructure projects putting severe  strain on Brazil's economy how bad did  it get Brazil's economy shrank 3.8  percent in 2015 the most in a quarter  century the country's credit rating was  downgraded to junk and business and  consumer confidence levels fell to  nearly their lowest on record  Rousseff's presidency has also been  strained by the massive scandal  involving Petrobras the state-run oil  company an investigation dubbed  Operation car wash revealed a deeply  corrupt organization and government it  unearthed to scheme started over a  decade ago involving bribery collusion  and kickbacks implicating Petrobras  executives and Brazilian officials  Rousseff herself has not been named in  the probe what might prove Rousseff son  doing however are claims she doctored  accounts to cover up the government's  deficit woes during her 2014 re-election  campaign in March more than three  million Brazilians took to the streets  calling for Rousseff's ouster on May  12th the Senate voted to put her on  trial if found guilty she would be  removed from office permanently  however Rousseff denies any wrong  now here is the argument those in the  anti Rousseff camp say that her time is  over  even if she survives her upcoming trial  which many say she won't a fragmented  government an angry public will prevent  her from accomplishing anything  meaningful the pro Rousseff camp say  Rousseff successor vice president Michel  temer isn't much better just last year  he tweeted quote impeachment is  unthinkable  since then Rousseff herself has accused  him of attempting a coup looking big  picture there aren't many good guys here  352 of Brazil's 594 members of Congress  are currently under investigation for  corruption charges brazil's future is  uncertain right now but whoever rises to  power next faces a tough task and  stitching the country back together
 

